In the photograph {\it work} is oilskin and
shovel - {\it staff} is clean ``white duster'' and
no tool and {\it up on the timber}.

That is the 2 figure group at $C/R$

* head counted $\approx 7$th head from the L.
$\approx 3$rd head from the absolute $R$ edge
$2$nd fully seen figure from the $R$

* the dark fedora/white duster with his hands
behind his back up on the stringer at $C/R$ and
his {\it foreman/mouthpiece} the moustached
white duster next to him to his $R$ with his $L$
arm in the air pointing at the crown - in
every day language ``the 2nd man from the $R$'' -
{\it viz} the only two men in clean light coats
standing posed up on the cross timber.

Normally head counted $L\to R$:

$$1={\rm back\;to\;camera\;L.\;edge}\;
2={\rm shovel\;in\;fore\;ground}\;
3={\rm behind\;him}\;
4/5 = {\rm two\;helmets\;on\;timber\;C.}\;
6={\rm small\;head\;behind\;them}\;
{\bf 7}={\bf dark\;hat/white\;duster\;hands\;behind\;
back\;on\;timber\;C/R- the\;visiting\;inspector/ \;
senior\;engineer}\;
8={\rm head\;peeping\;over\;his\;shoulder}\;
{\bf 9}={\bf moustached\;white\;duster\;arm\;up\;
on\;timber\;-\; his\;foreman\;{\rm -}\;normally\;
counted\;as\;``2nd\;from\;R''}\;
10={\rm half\;cropped\;tie/cap\;man\;extreme\;R.\;edge}$$

so $7\approx 8\,L\to R = 3\,R\to L = 2\,R\to L$
fully in frame.
